# Additional Malayalam pass criteria for the critique, mirroring the Malayalam hook
# priorities. Appended by prompts/critique_prompt.py::build_critique_prompt.

MALAYALAM_CRITIQUE = """

    ADDITIONAL MALAYALAM CRITERIA (a hook FAILS if any is not met):

    18. SINGLE FOCUS: focuses on exactly ONE jaw-dropping action, carried by ONE acting lead — FAILS if it mixes two different subplots or character actions into the same hook
    19. SHORT AND PUNCHY (OVERRIDES the length clause of criterion 14b): written as 2-3 sentences, every one short, simple and fast — FAILS if any sentence is long, heavy, or crowded. A long sentence fails here even when it carries a turn: in Malayalam the turn must be carried short. The shape itself is the writer's choice and is not grounds to fail; only the length and weight of the sentences are judged here
    20. SIMPLE AND DIRECT: written in plain, direct, everyday Malayalam — the words people actually speak, not written-register Malayalam. Read it aloud and judge it by ear: FAILS on literary or Sanskritised vocabulary where an everyday word exists, on any word a character in this story would not say out loud, and on any sentence that has to be heard twice to be followed
    21. LENGTH (OVERRIDES criterion 8): 2-3 sentences, 20-25 words — a hook longer than 25 words FAILS
    22. SCRIPT PURITY: every word is written in Malayalam script — FAILS if any English/Hindi/Tamil/Telugu/Kannada word is left untransliterated, or if a word is corrupted by characters from another script mixed into it. Exception: a character's exact name/nickname as it appears in the source chapter.
    23. MALAYALAM GRAMMAR: case endings (വിഭക്തി), verb tense/agreement, and sandhi (word joining) must all be correct standard Malayalam — FAILS on any case, agreement, or sandhi error, even a single one
    24. FORWARD FORM: the coming moment is carried by a forward form (...ും, ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു, ...ാനിരിക്കുന്നു) — FAILS if the target scene is narrated in the plain present (...ുന്നു, ...ുകയാണ്), which reads as though the listener were already standing inside a scene still chapters away from them. This is criterion 12 applied to Malayalam's own verb forms, not a separate rule. Judge it on the FINITE verb that carries the coming moment, taken one clause at a time — a participle or a nominalized form inside the sentence (കാത്തിരിക്കുന്ന, സംഭവിക്കുന്നത്) is not a failure as long as the clause's own finite verb is forward. Opening inside the coming moment is permitted by criterion 1b and does NOT excuse the plain present: ആ വാതിൽ വൈകാതെ തുറക്കും passes, ആ വാതിൽ തുറക്കുന്നു fails, and both are legitimate openings otherwise. The ground the reader already stands on is judged the opposite way: it is where the reader is standing right now, so an ongoing present there (...ുകയാണ്, ...ുന്നു) is CORRECT and must never be failed — that is the one place the plain present belongs. Note too that ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു and ...ാനിരിക്കുന്നു are forward forms even though പോകുന്നു and ഇരിക്കുന്നു look present: never fail them on this criterion
    25. CLOSING FORM: FAILS if the hook closes on a yes/no question — a line ending ...ഉമോ? or ...ആണോ? whose answer the listener can already guess (രക്ഷപ്പെടാൻ കഴിയുമോ?, തിരിച്ചറിയുമോ?), because the story plainly is not over. A question asking for a withheld identity (ആരാണ്?), a question naming two outcomes that both hurt, and a statement close all pass
    26. HONORIFIC SCRIPT: an English title attached to a name (Mr., Dr., Miss) is not part of the name and is not covered by criterion 22's exception — FAILS if it is left in Latin script rather than written in Malayalam (മിസ്റ്റർ, ഡോക്ടർ) or dropped
    28. THE FORWARD FORM HIDES THE OUTCOME (criterion 7b applied to Malayalam's own verb forms, not a separate rule): criterion 24 REQUIRES the coming moment to be carried by ...ും / ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു / ...ാനിരിക്കുന്നു, and a result written in those forms is still a result. Find the coming moment, then read every clause after it one at a time. FAILS if any of them says HOW IT WENT — what the moment led to, what it broke, who it hurt, how anyone answered it. The commonest shape is a third sentence of the form ആ + [the moment just named] + a consequence: ✗ ആ സാഹസം അവനെ വലിയൊരു ആപത്തിൽ എത്തിക്കും. ✗ മകളുടെ രഹസ്യ സ്വപ്നം ഇതോടെ തകരാൻ പോകുന്നു. ✗ ആ നിമിഷം ഡോക്ടറുടെ ജീവിതത്തെ പ്രതിസന്ധിയിലേക്ക് എത്തിക്കും. Each is correct Malayalam and each passes criterion 24; each fails here. Judge it clause by clause, never on the hook's overall feel, and never excuse it because 20-25 words left no room — the word limit is a reason to cut the ground, not the withholding. WHAT MUST NEVER BE FAILED HERE: the ground in the ongoing present (criterion 24 requires it); a stake named without its resolution (അപ്പന്റെ പേരുതന്നെയാണ് ആ സാഹസത്തിൽ പണയം); a certainty carried INTO the moment (അവൾ വഴങ്ങില്ലെന്ന ഉറപ്പോടെയാണ് അയാൾ ചെല്ലുന്നത്); a threshold image stopping at the instant before; and a closing question asking for the very thing withheld. The test is whether the clause tells the listener how it turned out, not whether anything appears after the moment at all.
    29. THE LIMIT IS NOT RELAXED BY MALAYALAM (criterion T1b applied here): nothing in criteria 18-28 permits naming more of the target scene than WHAT THIS PRECAP MUST HOLD BACK allows. FAILS if the hook names an object, person, act or motive that limit told it to hold back — the short length, the three-beat spine and the demand for a concrete physical arrival are never a defence, because all three can be satisfied on the ground the chapter just read already gave the reader. Then apply the retelling test, which fails a hook even when it has stayed inside the limit: could a listener who heard only this precap retell the coming scene? If they could, it was delivered rather than hinted at, and it FAILS — a Malayalam precap points at one thing and stops, and a precap that stacks the people, the place and the turn into its 20-25 words has stacked the whole scene. This is not a licence to fail a vague hook: criterion 4 still requires one seeable thing, and one is exactly what passes here.
    27. SENTENCES CONNECT: every sentence after the first picks up something the previous one named — through a pronoun (അവൾ, അയാൾ), ആ + an already-named noun, a place already given (അവിടെ), or a carrying opener (അതോടെ, അപ്പോൾ). FAILS if a sentence restarts by repeating the character's full name where NOTHING WAS AMBIGUOUS, if a middle sentence only restates the previous one from another angle instead of advancing it, or if the closing line drops the concrete thing just named for a general line that would fit another chapter equally well. Then check every അവൾ, അയാൾ and ആ + noun separately: how many people in the precap could it mean? More than one — FAIL, and say in the reason that the name belongs there. Exactly one — correct, leave it. Two women both take അവൾ and two men both take അയാൾ, so a precap carrying two leads of the same gender is where this breaks. A name repeated to keep two characters apart NEVER fails this criterion, however often it repeats; only a name repeated where one person was ever in question does. Judge the linking only — this is not a licence to fail a hook for short sentences, which criterion 19 requires
    """
